## Artifact chain

| Artifact | Location | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Concept | `docs/concept.md` | |
| Requirements | `docs/requirements.md` | |
| Architecture | `docs/architecture.md` | |
| Decisions | `docs/adr/` | |
| Work tracking | GitHub issues | |

**Always read the relevant ADRs before proposing anything architectural.** They record *why*,
which is the context most often missing.

## Conventions

### ADRs
- Location: `docs/adr/NNNN-kebab-case-title.md`, zero-padded to 4 digits
- Template: `docs/adr/0000-template.md`
- **ADRs are living documents.** Edit one in place when understanding improves, and add a
  Revisions line. Write a new record only when the *problem* changed, not the answer — then link
  `Replaces` / `Replaced by` in both directions
- Status is `Exploring | Working | Revisiting | Replaced`. There is no `Accepted` and no `Final`
- Every ADR links to the issue that prompted it, and carries a **Cost of change**
- When you touch an area, glance at its ADR: still true → bump `Last reviewed`; not true → fix it

### Issues
- Labels: `type:*` (feature, bug, chore, spike), `area:*`, `blocked`
- An issue implementing a decision links to its ADR in the body
- Definition of done lives in the issue, not in someone's head

### Commits & PRs
- Conventional commits: `feat(scope): …`, `fix(scope): …`, `docs(scope): …`
- PR body links the issue and any ADR it implements

