## Settled decisions

Things already decided — do not re-litigate these casually. Each record's *What would change our
mind* section says what a legitimate reopening looks like; if you've hit one of those conditions,
that's a cue to reopen it rather than work around it quietly. Link the ADR.

- <decision> — ADR-000N
- <decision> — ADR-000N

## Working agreements for Claude

- **Read state from the repo.** ADRs and open issues are the source of truth, not this conversation.
- **Write artifacts back to the repo.** Don't leave conclusions only in chat.
- **Surface guesses.** When producing any artifact, end it with an
  `## Open questions I had to answer myself` section listing every assumption made because the
  upstream artifact was underspecified. This is how upstream problems get caught early.
- **Don't invent requirements.** If something is missing, say so rather than filling the gap.
- **Ask before scope expansion.** Doing more than asked is a cost, not a bonus.
- **Hard gates:** do not proceed past requirements or architecture without explicit human approval.

## Skills

This repo's pack: `<meta | go-service | cloudflare-worker | web-ui | marketing-site>`
(see the registry in ai-workbench `packs.md`).

Blessed — vendored in `.claude/skills/`, one line each on why:

| Skill | Why it's here |
|---|---|
| `<name>` | `<the job it does in this repo>` |

Deliberately absent — so nobody re-adds them:

- `<name>` — `<why this repo doesn't need it>`

Skills are installed per repo, not globally. A global copy of the same name silently overrides the
one here, so if a vendored skill isn't behaving, check `~/.claude/skills/` first.

Anything that writes, pushes, deletes or spends carries `disable-model-invocation: true` and is
invoked by hand. The test isn't "is it dangerous" but "would I be surprised to find it had
happened?"

## Commands available

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `/adr` | Create a numbered ADR from the template |
| `/issues-from-adr` | Propose GitHub issues implementing a decision |
